// @emit-error monomorphize: unsupported argument type for `self` in a call to `sorted`
// THE REACH OF `std:array`'S GENERIC SURFACE, pinned where a caller will meet it.
//
// `sorted<T>` — and `sort<T>`, `reverse<T>`, `indexOf<T>`, and every other export —
// admits scalars (`i32`/`i64`/`f64`/`boolean`), `string`, and nested lists (`i32[][]`),
// all measured — and literal unions, which this comment has now been wrong about in BOTH
// directions: it first claimed they worked, was corrected to "NOT literal unions — that is
// the miscompile next door, `xfail-miscompile-array-litunion-element.vl`", and that file has
// since graduated (`tests/cases/std/array-sorted.vl` carries the row). `std/array.vl`'s
// header holds the current reach and the exports still outside it.
//
// WHAT THIS FIXTURE PINS IS THE RECEIVER'S **SCOPE**, AND THE SENTENCE THAT STOOD HERE SAID
// THE ELEMENT. It read "what has never moved is that it does NOT admit a list whose element is
// a STRUCT", and that is false and was false the whole time: the same four-row `Row[]` built
// INSIDE A FUNCTION sorts correctly and stably, `reverse` runs, and `indexOf` runs with
// structural struct equality. `tests/cases/std/array-struct-element-function-scope.vl` is that
// capability, pinned, and it passes on `235b365b` too — so this comment was costing a caller
// something the compiler already did. It is `std/array.vl`'s ledger's FIRST entry in the
// restrictive direction; every other one over-promised. Found by the `std-api-reviewer` pass
// over the D33 retirement.
//
// The axis, measured cell by cell: a module-scope BINDING is refused (this file), and so is
// the same binding read from inside a function — it is the binding's scope, not the call's —
// while a function-local list and a call RESULT used directly as the receiver
// (`mk().sorted(byR)`, even at top level) both run.
//
// The monomorphizer refuses the instantiation at EMIT time, so `vl check` reports the
// program clean and only `vl build`/`vl run` says no.
//
// This is a PRE-EXISTING, module-wide limitation of the monomorphizer, not something
// `sorted` introduced: `tests/cases/generics/error-genapp-tyaram-*.vl` pin the same
// refusal on hand-written generics, and swapping `sorted` for `reverse` below produces
// the same message with the same shape. It is pinned HERE as well because it decides
// what `std:array` can be used for, and because sorting records by a field is the
// single most obvious thing a caller will reach for first.
//
// THE INDEX-LIST IDIOM IS THE ANSWER FOR A MODULE-SCOPE TABLE — not, as this comment used to
// imply, for sorting records at all: sort an INDEX list and
// let the comparator read the keys out of parallel arrays it closes over. That is what
// `array-sorted-stability.vl` does, what `compiler/cli.vl` already does twice, and it
// is available precisely because `less` is a plain `(T, T) => boolean` closure rather
// than a key extractor.
//
// When the monomorphizer grows struct elements, this fixture fails loudly — which is
// the point of pinning it. The fix is to delete this file and add the struct case to
// `array-sorted-stability.vl`, where a `{ key, seq }` record is the natural spelling.
import { sorted } from "std:array"

type Row = { key: i32, seq: i32 }

const rows = [{ key: 2, seq: 0 }, { key: 1, seq: 1 }]
print(rows.sorted((a: Row, b: Row) => a.key < b.key).length)
